DOGE-5 — Steve Davis and the Alleged Shadow-Management Structure Behind DOGE Operations

I. Purpose of Exhibit

This exhibit examines the reported role of Steve Davis, a longtime Elon Musk associate and former senior executive of The Boring Company, in the organization, staffing, coordination, and daily operation of the Department of Government Efficiency.

The term “shadow-management structure” refers to the hypothesis that Davis and other Musk-associated personnel exercised practical authority without occupying the publicly identified positions established by Executive Order 14158.

The controlling factual questions are:

Did Davis hold a federal appointment?
What was his title, duty station, and period of service?
Was he an employee, SGE, detailee, consultant, volunteer, or unofficial adviser?
Who supervised him?
Did he possess authority to direct DOGE personnel?
Did agencies treat his communications as governmental directives?
Did he recruit personnel from Musk’s private-business network?
Did he participate in contract, grant, employment, property, or data-access decisions?
Did he continue influencing DOGE after leaving government?
Did his conduct connect private interests to qualifying RICO predicate activity?

Public reporting justifies further investigation. It does not, by itself, prove that Davis secretly controlled DOGE or participated in racketeering.

II. Steve Davis’s Relationship With Elon Musk

Davis has been associated professionally with Musk for many years.

His reported roles have included:

Senior leadership at The Boring Company;

Work connected to SpaceX;
Participation in cost-reduction or restructuring efforts within Musk-controlled businesses;
Assistance during Musk’s acquisition and restructuring of Twitter, later renamed X;
Participation in personnel and expense reviews;
Service as a trusted operational associate; and
Subsequent involvement in the DOGE initiative.
This history is relevant because DOGE reportedly used management methods similar to those employed during Musk’s restructuring of Twitter/X, including:

Rapid cost reviews;

Broad requests for employee information;
Staffing reductions;
Short decision deadlines;
Centralized approval processes;
Use of small teams of trusted personnel;
High reliance on digital systems and spreadsheets;
Review of vendor and lease expenses; and
Demands that employees justify their continued positions or activities.
Similar management methods do not establish a criminal scheme. They may explain why Musk selected Davis to assist with DOGE.

III. Reported DOGE Role

Public reporting described Davis as one of DOGE’s most influential operational figures and, in some accounts, as the person coordinating daily activity below Musk.

Reported responsibilities attributed to Davis included:

Recruiting DOGE personnel;

Interviewing or evaluating prospective participants;

Coordinating teams assigned to federal agencies;
Reviewing proposed spending reductions;
Receiving information from embedded personnel;
Participating in workforce-reduction planning;
Communicating operational priorities;
Managing logistical or personnel issues;
Coordinating with Musk;
Maintaining contact with agency-based DOGE personnel; and
Helping identify successors or continuing leaders before departing government.
These descriptions must be authenticated through government records and testimony. Anonymous-source reporting can identify leads but should not be treated as conclusive proof of Davis’s authority.
IV. Absence From the Published Formal Hierarchy

Executive Order 14158 did not establish a position for Davis.

The formal order identified:

The President;

The White House Chief of Staff;

The USDS Administrator;

Agency heads;
Agency DOGE team leads; and
Agency DOGE team members.
Amy Gleason declared that she supervised all USDS employees and detailees assigned to USDS. She did not identify Davis as a superior or as someone to whom she reported.
This creates the following possibilities:
A. Davis worked within Gleason’s formal authority

Under this explanation, Davis was an employee or temporary official working under the USDS Administrator, even if his public title and duties were not initially disclosed.

B. Davis worked through Musk’s White House advisory office

Davis may have assisted Musk rather than USDS, transmitting information or recommendations from Musk to properly appointed officials.

C. Davis operated through an agency appointment

Davis may have been appointed to a position at an agency such as GSA or detailed through another component.

D. Davis served as an informal or volunteer adviser

He may have supplied advice without possessing governmental decision-making authority.

E. Davis exercised practical authority outside the formal chain

Under the shadow-management hypothesis, Davis acted as an operational manager while Gleason remained the nominal USDS Administrator and agency officials formally approved the resulting decisions.

Appointment, payroll, security, email, badge, directory, and communication records are required to resolve these possibilities.

V. Relationship With Amy Gleason

The most important command question is whether Davis reported to, coordinated with, bypassed, or practically outranked Gleason.

Investigators should determine:

Whether Gleason approved Davis’s participation;

Whether Davis appeared on the USDS personnel roster;

Whether Davis attended USDS leadership meetings;

Whether Gleason assigned him responsibilities;

Whether he gave instructions to USDS employees;
Whether he received reports from agency DOGE teams;
Whether he communicated with Wiles independently of Gleason;
Whether Gleason reviewed or approved his decisions;
Whether their responsibilities overlapped;
Whether Davis managed operations while Gleason performed concurrent HHS duties;
Whether Gleason understood Davis to represent Musk, USDS, the President, or OMB; and
Whether disagreements between Gleason and Davis were escalated to Wiles or Trump.
The existence of operational responsibilities delegated by Gleason would not constitute shadow management. The material issue is whether Davis exercised authority without disclosure, appointment, supervision, or lawful delegation.
VI. Relationship With Musk
Davis’s longstanding association with Musk creates a plausible private chain through which Musk could have communicated with DOGE personnel while remaining formally outside USDS.
The possible structure requiring investigation is:

