    A["Elon Musk<br/>White House adviser"] --> B["Steve Davis<br/>Reported operational intermediary"]
    B -. "Disputed practical direction" .-> C["USDS and DOGE personnel"]
    C --> D["Agency DOGE teams"]
    D --> E["Agency actions"]
    F["Amy Gleason<br/>Formal USDS Administrator"] --> C

The dotted connection is an investigative hypothesis.

Investigators must determine whether Davis:

Regularly briefed Musk;

Received priorities or approvals from Musk;

Transmitted Musk’s instructions to DOGE personnel;
Reported agency resistance to Musk;
Used Musk-controlled communication infrastructure;
Coordinated DOGE personnel formerly employed by Musk companies;
Continued communicating with Musk after Musk’s formal departure; and
Treated Musk rather than Gleason or Wiles as the ultimate operational authority.
A close professional relationship does not establish unlawful direction.
VII. DOGE Personnel Recruitment

One of Davis’s most significant reported responsibilities involved identifying and recruiting DOGE personnel.

The recruitment process requires investigation because DOGE participants reportedly included:

Engineers;
Technology executives;
Venture-capital personnel;
Former employees of Musk-associated companies;
Young technical personnel;
Special government employees;
Detailees;
Full-time employees;

Volunteers; and

Individuals subsequently placed in senior agency positions.

For every recruit, investigators should determine:

Who identified the person;

Who interviewed the person;

Who approved the appointment;
Whether Davis participated;
Whether Musk approved the selection;
What legal appointment was used;
What background investigation occurred;
What clearance or system access was provided;
Which agency employed the person;
Which official supervised the person;
Whether the person retained outside employment;
Whether a financial disclosure was filed;

Whether ethics and records training occurred;

Whether the person communicated through private accounts; and
Whether the person continued in government after Davis and Musk departed.
Recruitment from a known professional network is not inherently improper. It becomes relevant if participants were chosen to maintain private loyalty, evade ordinary appointment rules, access sensitive information, or advance undisclosed financial objectives.
VIII. Potential Musk-Loyalty Network
Public reporting described numerous DOGE participants as having professional connections to:
Musk;
SpaceX;
Tesla;
X;
The Boring Company;
xAI;
Palantir;
Silicon Valley venture-capital firms;
Technology startups; and
People within Davis’s professional network.

This creates a personnel-screening issue but not proof of a criminal association.

Investigators must distinguish among:

Ordinary professional referrals;

Shared technical expertise;

Political alignment;
Personal loyalty;
Government employment;
Undisclosed private direction;
Coordinated commercial interests; and
Agreement to participate in unlawful activity.
RICO liability cannot be inferred merely because several participants previously worked for the same private employer.
IX. GSA as a Possible Operational Hub
The General Services Administration occupied a strategically important position in DOGE’s work because GSA oversees or supports:
Federal procurement;

Governmentwide technology services;

Federal buildings and leases;

Acquisition systems;
Government email and collaboration systems;
Federal property disposal;
Vendor relationships;
Payment and contract information; and
Administrative services used throughout the executive branch.
Public reporting later described Davis as influential in selecting or supporting DOGE-aligned officials within GSA.
If accurate, GSA could have served as an operational hub through which DOGE implemented:

Lease terminations;

Property sales;

Contract reviews;

Technology procurement;

Governmentwide personnel support;
Centralized data analysis;
Vendor reductions; and
Placement of DOGE-associated officials.
Investigators should determine whether Davis held a GSA appointment or exercised influence through GSA officials.
X. Selection of Continuing DOGE Leadership
Public reporting alleged that before Musk and Davis departed government, Davis participated in selecting or supporting officials who would continue DOGE-related work.
Reportedly relevant individuals included officials placed in or connected with GSA leadership. The precise appointments, titles, timing, and selection process must be verified through official records.
The relevant questions include:

Did Davis recommend successors?

Did he possess appointment authority?

Did Gleason or Wiles approve the selections?
Did the President approve them?
Were candidates selected because of loyalty to Musk or because of technical competence?
Did Davis continue directing them after leaving government?
Did they retain communications with Musk?
Did they occupy positions with independent statutory authority?
Did their placement preserve DOGE policies after Musk’s departure?
Did any private company benefit from their decisions?

Succession planning is ordinary organizational conduct. It becomes probative only if it was intended to preserve an undisclosed private command structure or facilitate predicate offenses.

XI. Reported Post-Departure Influence

Davis reportedly left government around the time Musk’s formal service ended in late May 2025.

Subsequent reporting alleged that Davis attempted to preserve influence over DOGE-aligned personnel or GSA leadership after leaving.

If confirmed, post-departure conduct would require separating:

Lawful informal advice;

Political advocacy;
Communication with former colleagues;
Representation of a private employer before the government;
Unauthorized direction of federal personnel;
Use of nonpublic government information;
Attempts to influence contracting or personnel matters; and
Continued participation in an alleged enterprise.
The following evidence would be decisive:
Communications after departure;
Meeting records;

Visitor logs;

Calls with agency officials;

Private-company employment records;

Compensation;

Matters discussed;

Federal decisions following those communications; and

Any representation or lobbying disclosures required by law.
XII. Signal and Collaborative Documents
American Oversight filed litigation naming DOGE, Musk, Davis, Gleason, and other officials regarding the alleged use of Signal and Google Docs for government business.
The complaint alleges systematic records-law violations. Those remain allegations unless established through evidence or judicial findings.
The litigation identifies several important investigative questions:
Did Davis conduct DOGE business through Signal?
Were disappearing-message settings enabled?
Were messages forwarded to official recordkeeping systems?

Did Davis use a personal telephone or account?

Did he create or edit collaborative documents directing DOGE activity?
Were document histories preserved?
Did DOGE personnel use private workspaces unavailable to agency records officers?
Were litigation holds issued?
Were messages deleted after preservation obligations arose?
Did any person intend to prevent investigators or the public from reconstructing DOGE decisions?
Use of Signal or Google Docs is not automatically unlawful or obstructive. The legal question depends on record status, preservation procedures, intent, and nexus to a qualifying proceeding.
Source: American Oversight DOGE records litigation
XIII. Contract and Grant Terminations

DOGE publicly took credit for large numbers of contract, grant, and lease terminations.

Investigators must determine Davis’s role in:

Selecting awards for review;

Developing termination criteria;

Preparing lists of targeted contracts;

Communicating with agency contracting officers;
Calculating purported savings;
Reviewing contractor descriptions;
Directing rapid approvals;
Removing entries from the Wall of Receipts;
Correcting erroneous calculations; and
Selecting replacement contractors or service providers.
For each action, the record should identify:
The originating official;
The recommendation;

The legal authority;

The contracting officer;

Agency-head approval;

Davis’s involvement;

Musk’s involvement;

Any private interest affected;

Actual savings or costs; and
Any benefit to an associated entity.
Even improperly directed termination activity is not automatically a RICO predicate.
XIV. Workforce Reductions
Davis’s private-sector experience reportedly included aggressive cost and staffing reductions.
Investigators should determine whether he participated in:
The deferred-resignation program;
The “Fork in the Road” communication;
Requests that federal employees describe weekly accomplishments;
Probationary-employee terminations;

Agency reduction-in-force planning;

Identification of positions for elimination;
Selection of personnel placed on administrative leave;
Removal of officials who resisted DOGE access;
Rehiring employees mistakenly terminated; and
Conversion of DOGE personnel from SGEs to full-time appointments.
A lawful or unlawful employment action is not ordinarily a RICO predicate. It becomes relevant if used to:
Extort property;
Retaliate for protected information concerning federal crimes;
Obstruct an investigation;
Facilitate fraudulent access;

Install participants in a bribery scheme; or

Conceal an unlawful enterprise.

XV. System and Data Access

If Davis directed or coordinated DOGE technical personnel, his role may extend to requests for access to federal information systems.

Investigators should determine whether he:

Selected databases for DOGE review;
Approved requests for administrator-level access;
Received reports derived from sensitive data;
Possessed direct access credentials;
Ordered bulk data transfers;
Directed data centralization;
Approved use of commercial AI tools;
Authorized data exports to external systems;
Received personally identifiable information;
Communicated with Musk-controlled companies about government data; or

Participated in the installation or modification of agency software.

There is no public evidence established here showing that Davis transferred government data to a private company. The issue requires forensic evidence.
XVI. Financial and Private-Interest Analysis
Davis’s private affiliations require matter-specific examination.
Relevant interests may include:
The Boring Company;
Musk-controlled companies;

Investments or equity interests;

Compensation arrangements;

Deferred compensation;

Venture-capital relationships;

Consulting work;
Private-company contracts with the government; and
Commercial interests affected by DOGE actions.
Investigators must obtain:
Financial disclosures;
Ethics agreements;
Recusals;
Section 208 waivers;
Outside-employment approvals;
Equity records;
Compensation records; and

Communications concerning affected companies.

Private employment or equity does not establish self-dealing. Investigators must connect an identifiable financial interest to Davis’s personal and substantial participation in a particular government matter.

XVII. Shadow-Management Indicators

The following circumstances support investigating a possible shadow-management structure:

Davis’s longstanding status as a trusted Musk operational associate;

Public reporting identifying him as a senior DOGE operator;
Uncertainty surrounding his formal title and appointment;
Musk’s formal position outside USDS;
Gleason’s claim to supervise USDS;
The delay in publicly identifying Gleason;
Gleason’s concurrent HHS responsibilities;
DOGE’s distributed agency-team system;
Use of private or ephemeral communication platforms alleged in litigation;
Recruitment from Musk-associated networks;

Possible involvement in succession planning;

Continued influence alleged after formal departure;
Agency decisions following DOGE communications; and
The difficulty of attributing individual DOGE decisions to a named official.
These are indicators requiring examination. They do not prove concealment or criminal control.
XVIII. Lawful and Exculpatory Explanations
The investigation must test competing explanations, including:
Davis was properly appointed;
He worked under Gleason’s supervision;

He possessed no decision-making authority;

He merely coordinated information;

Agency heads independently approved all final actions;

His recruitment role was limited to identifying qualified technical personnel;

Musk’s trust in Davis reflected professional competence;
GSA officials were independently selected by the President;
Post-departure communications involved ordinary advice;
Private messages were preserved appropriately;
No federal information was transferred to private companies;
Davis held no financial interest affected by his work;
Contract and personnel decisions reflected administration policy;
The perceived shadow structure resulted from DOGE’s decentralized design; and
Public reporting overstated his authority.
XIX. Potential RICO Predicate Analysis
A. Wire or mail fraud
A possible theory would require proof that Davis knowingly participated in a property-focused scheme using electronic or postal communications.
False descriptions of his title or authority would not be sufficient unless connected to obtaining money or property.
B. Honest-services fraud

A theory would require evidence of bribery or kickbacks. Undisclosed influence, divided loyalty, or favoritism alone is insufficient.

C. Hobbs Act extortion

Investigators would need evidence that Davis used actual or apparent governmental power to obtain transferable property for himself or another participant.

Pressuring agencies to reduce spending does not by itself satisfy the obtaining-property requirement.

D. Obstruction
The use or deletion of Signal messages could become relevant if Davis acted corruptly to obstruct a foreseeable or pending qualifying federal proceeding.
A records-management violation is not automatically obstruction.
E. Witness or informant retaliation
A predicate could exist if Davis participated in prohibited retaliation against a person for supplying truthful information about a possible federal crime.
Ordinary employment disputes or political retaliation do not necessarily fall within the enumerated statute.
F. Computer-related offenses
Unauthorized access to government systems may implicate the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act. However, investigators must separately determine whether the specific offense qualifies as a RICO predicate and whether Davis knowingly directed or joined it.
G. RICO conspiracy
Investigators must establish that Davis agreed that members would conduct enterprise affairs through a pattern of predicate offenses.
Agreement to help Musk implement DOGE is not sufficient.
XX. Required Investigative Materials
Davis’s appointment letter;
His job title and position description;
SGE, employee, detailee, consultant, or volunteer designation;

Payroll and time records;

Beginning and ending service dates;

Security-clearance and background-investigation records;

Government badge and facility-access logs;

Government email and telephone assignments;

System-access credentials;

Financial-disclosure records;

Ethics training and counseling records;

Recusals and Section 208 waivers;

Communications with Musk;

Communications with Gleason;

Communications with Wiles;

Communications with Trump or presidential staff;

Communications with OMB;

Communications with agency DOGE teams;

Communications with GSA officials;

Communications with former Musk-company employees;

DOGE recruitment and interview records;

Personnel-selection spreadsheets;

Agency-assignment records;

Contract and grant termination lists;

Workforce-reduction documents;

Deferred-resignation communications;

Weekly-accomplishment email records;
Wall of Receipts drafts and revision histories;
Signal account and preservation records;
Google Docs ownership and revision histories;
Litigation-hold notices;
Data-access and export logs;
Private-company communications;
Post-departure communications with government officials;
Compensation and employment records after departure;
Succession-planning documents;
Records concerning GSA leadership selections;
Testimony from Davis, Gleason, Wiles, Musk, and agency heads;
Evidence showing recommendations Davis made that were rejected; and
Evidence of any private financial benefit connected to a government action.
XXI. Preliminary Investigative Finding
The public record provides a reasonable basis to investigate whether Steve Davis served as a principal operational intermediary connecting Musk to DOGE personnel and federal agencies.
His longstanding relationship with Musk, reported recruitment role, alleged daily operational involvement, connection to DOGE-aligned personnel, uncertain formal position, and alleged post-departure influence are relevant to determining DOGE’s actual chain of command.
The evidence presently reviewed does not establish that Davis:
Secretly controlled USDS;
Superseded Amy Gleason;
Possessed unlawful access to government systems;
Transferred government information to private companies;
Directed criminal retaliation;
Obtained federal property;
Received bribes or kickbacks;
Destroyed federal records with corrupt intent; or
Agreed to conduct DOGE’s affairs through racketeering predicates.
The RICO theory requires the following evidentiary chain:
Davis’s operational role → actual direction of DOGE personnel → undisclosed private or unlawful purpose → qualifying predicate conduct → knowing coordination with Musk or other enterprise participants.
Without that chain, the evidence may demonstrate an opaque and unconventional management arrangement without establishing a criminal enterprise.

